  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Patchy fog expected Sunday morning. \n\n- Warm, dry week ahead for the region.\n\n- Showers return late next week or early next weekend.\n\nThe recent rainfall combined with decreasing cloud cover and\nstable conditions the next few days will cause patchy fog in many\nof the valleys of central Idaho and western Montana Sunday\nmorning. The fog is expected to clear out by Sunday afternoon due\nto drier air from aloft mixing down in the afternoon. \n\nAs the cutoff low over the region moves out, another is moving\ninto the U.S. West Coast and heading south of the desert\nsouthwest. There's also a broad trough established across the\nnorthern Pacific Ocean. The two features combined will setup a\nsouthwest, split flow pattern into the northern Rockies region. As\na result, the region will see warm and mild conditions most of the\nweek. Afternoon temperatures will be about 10 degrees above season\naverages with light winds and partly cloudy skies. \n\nAs the broad trough over the northern Pacific starts to progress\neastward, it will start to bring showers back to the region late\nnext week or early next weekend. With the southwest flow pattern,\nthe snow levels will remain very high and mountain snow isn't a\nconcern yet, however, as we get into the week after if the trough\ncontinues to progress eastward it would bring some cooler air into\nthe region. So the bottom line is expect a warm, mild week with\nsome showers moving in next weekend and the potential for more\nmountain snow a week or more away.",
